  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n500mb ridging will be occasionally reinforced over the region. An \nupper low moving north into New England will further slow the \nability for this ridge to eject. This ridge is most likely to exit\nin the mid portion of next week, eventually returning rain \nchances. But very quiet before then. \n\nSprawling surface high pressure over eastern Canada will contribute \nto a persistent dry airmass here in northern MI. A weaker area of \nhigh pressure lingers over the Great Lakes early next week, after \nthe east coast system moves north. And /that/ finally gets displaced \nby a cold front midweek next week.\n\nHealthy cu field over ne lower MI, as diurnal heating interacts goes \nto work on lake-moistened air moving inland on east low-level winds. \nThose clouds fade away this evening with/after sunset. Another cool \nnight tonight, with some mid/upper 30s in the interior cool spots in \neastern upper and n central lower MI. Perhaps not quite as chilly \n(and not quite as much frost) as this morning; surface dew points \nhave rebounded well into the 40s/near 50f.\n\nTemp trends will be subtle moving forward. A hair warmer Thursday \ncompared to today, and then relatively steady into the weekend. A \nbrief pre-fropa temp bump is possible early next week, perhaps \nTuesday.",
